```jsx
import React, { useRef, useLayoutEffect } from "react";

function MyComponent() {
  const ref = useRef(null);

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    // Get the size of the element after it has been rendered
    const width = ref.current.offsetWidth;
    const height = ref.current.offsetHeight;
    console.log(`The element is ${width}px wide and ${height}px tall.`);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div ref={ref}>
      This is my element.
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, we're using the `useRef` hook to create a reference to a `div` element. We're then using the `useLayoutEffect` hook to measure the size of the element after it has been rendered.

Since `useLayoutEffect` is called synchronously after all DOM mutations, we can be sure that the `ref.current.offsetWidth` and `ref.current.offsetHeight` properties will give us the correct values for the element's width and height.

By passing an empty array as the second argument to `useLayoutEffect`, we're telling React to only call this effect once, when the component is mounted. This means that we'll only log the size of the element once, when it is first rendered.

In React, the `ref` prop is a way to get a reference to a specific instance of a component or DOM element in your React application.

When you use the `ref` prop on a DOM element, React will create a reference to that element that you can access in your component's code. You can create a `ref` using the `useRef` hook or by creating a `React.createRef()` instance.

For example, consider the following code:
```jsx
function MyComponent() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  function handleClick() {
    inputRef.current.focus();
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <input type="text" ref={inputRef} />
      <button onClick={handleClick}>Focus input</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, we're creating a `ref` using the `useRef` hook and assigning it to the `ref` prop of an `input` element. We're also creating a `handleClick` function that will be called when the user clicks a button.

When the `handleClick` function is called, it will call the `focus()` method on the `inputRef.current` property, which will give focus to the input element.

The `ref` prop can also be used to get a reference to a specific instance of a custom component that you've defined in your React application. When you create a custom component, you can use the `React.forwardRef` function to forward the `ref` prop to a child component, which can then use the `ref` to get a reference to a specific instance of the child component.

Overall, the `ref` prop in React is a powerful tool that allows you to interact with the DOM and specific instances of components in your application.

